  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening the response of the Catholic Church to HIV and AIDS  &#x2018;Esperanza de VIHda&#x2019; Campaign</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Central America, Mexico and Panama have some of the highest rates of HIV and AIDS across Latin America. Mexico has about 10% of the total number of HIV cases in the continent (186,000 PLWHAs, according to CENSIDA, 30 June 2007) and AIDS (112,830 reported cases: 19,378 female and 93,452 male, CENSIDA 30 June 2007) is now the fourth cause of death amongst the young Mexican male population. Stigma and discrimination towards those affected by and living with the virus is in large part the result of ignorance and misunderstanding about HIV and the Church and FBOs play a central part in challenging such attitudes. In recent years, the role of the Catholic Church has become stronger and more visible, as church leaders have recognized the devastating social and economic impact of the pandemic in their parishes and dioceses. CAMEXPA, the region-wide network of Caritas in Central America, Mexico and Panama, has identified HIV and AIDS as one of its priority areas of work and, as a result, has begun to organize and participate in capacity building and strategic planning events on this theme. In May 2007, the Latin American Bishops gathered in Aparecida, Brazil and renewed their commitment and priority to accompany PLWHAs (Ap.#421).   

CEPS-C&#xe1;ritas Mexico is organizing a series of activities on behalf of people living with HIV and AIDS and their families at the diocesan and national level as part of an awareness raising campaign called Esperanza de VIHda (Hope of HIV/Life &#x2013; a play on words in Spanish), which begun in 2004. Activities include work with pastoral workers, priests and bishops to increase levels of awareness and involvement in HIV prevention activities, the organisation of an annual National Forum and accompaniment to the National network of FBOs working on HIV and AIDS (RedFe), as well as advocacy work at national and international levels.</narrative>
  </description>
